exit: 0
--- stdout
aethyme mcp — start MCP servers without launcher chains (opt-in)

Entries live in <host state>/mcp/services.toml and, per repository, in
.aethyme/mcp.toml (schema = 1, one [[servers]] table each): name, mode
(shared|direct), transport (stdio|http), argv, cwd, env_file, env_inherit
(default|none), env_allow, source, flavour, allow_cached. Paths must name
stable copies: an entry pointing into a broker session worktree is
refused, because sessions are cleaned up. A host entry always wins over a
repository entry of the same name; repository entries may only name paths
inside their repository and run only after `aethyme mcp trust`. Nothing is
written to any agent configuration; the owner pastes the snippet `config
print` shows.

Usage:
  aethyme mcp resolve <name> [--from-worktree] [--json]
      Resolve the entry's launcher chain (pnpm exec, npm exec, npx) to the
      installed executable and print the direct argv, the executable's
      identity (path, size, mtime, script interpreter), the chosen copy and
      the key. Runs nothing and never downloads: when no installed copy is
      found offline it says so, keeps the declared argv and exits 6. A
      changed executable since the last resolution is reported.
  aethyme mcp connect <name> [--from-worktree] [--fallback-cwd <dir>]
                      [--fallback -- <command...>]
      Replace this process with the resolved server (exec): stdio passes
      straight through and no extra process remains. Inside a broker session
      worktree it uses that worktree's copy when its `source` or lockfiles
      differ from the stable copy, or with --from-worktree; otherwise the
      stable copy. It prints `aethyme mcp: using <stable|worktree> copy, key
      <short>` on stderr. Any failure before the exec exits non-zero, unless
      --fallback is given: then the fallback command runs instead (in
      --fallback-cwd when given), so an agent is never blocked.
  aethyme mcp trust [--repo <path>] [--yes --digest <sha256>] [--json]
      Print what the repository's .aethyme/mcp.toml declares (each entry's
      argv, cwd, env_file, env_allow) and its sha256, then trust that exact
      file on this host after a typed confirmation on a terminal, or with
      --yes --digest <the printed sha256>. Any change needs trust again.
  aethyme mcp status [--json]
      Every entry with its resolution, identity, chosen copy and key,
      refused, shadowed and untrusted entries, and per-entry counters from
      <host state>/mcp/events.jsonl (outcomes, failure reasons, resolve
      time). Writes nothing but the per-host key secret on first use.
  aethyme mcp config print --agent codex|claude [--name <name>]
      Print the configuration snippet for each entry (or one) in that
      agent's format: `aethyme mcp connect <name> --fallback -- <argv>`.
      Prints only; never writes a file.
  aethyme mcp hub serve|launch <directory>
      Internal: the shared hub for mode = shared stdio entries, launched
      detached by the first `connect` that needs it (never at boot). One
      upstream server per key, multiplexed; it exits when nothing has used
      it for a while. A client that cannot share starts a private server.

Managed servers (host file only: managed = true, match_packages,
match_executables, repair = auto|report, args = caller|pinned):
  aethyme mcp manage list [--json]
  aethyme mcp manage add <name> --package <pkg> [--package ...]
                         [--executable <name> ...] [--repair auto|report]
                         [--args caller|pinned] [--json]
  aethyme mcp manage remove <name> [--json]
      List, mark or unmark managed entries in <host state>/mcp/services.toml
      (edited in place, formatting kept). A new entry starts as
      `npx -y <first package>`. Refreshes the shims when interception is on.
      args = caller (default): the caller's arguments follow the entry's
      argv and are part of the key; pinned: entry arguments only, and a
      caller with other arguments is left alone.
  aethyme mcp connect <name> --caller-args <n> [--caller-package <spec>]
                      --fallback -- <command...>
      The form interception and repair write: the last <n> words of the
      fallback command are the caller's arguments (without --fallback, the
      <n> words after `--`); --caller-package replaces the entry's package
      spec with the caller's pinned version.
  aethyme mcp intercept install [--dir <path>] [--json]
  aethyme mcp intercept uninstall [--json]
  aethyme mcp intercept status [--json]
      Shims named after npx, npm, pnpm, pnpx, bunx, yarn and each managed
      executable, pointing at aethyme: a matching call from an agent host
      becomes `aethyme mcp connect <name> --caller-args <n> --fallback --
      <real launcher> <args>`; anything else (and --help, -h, --version, a
      terminal on stdin) runs the real tool untouched. Prints the PATH line
      to add for agent hosts; never edits a shell profile.
  aethyme mcp repair [--check|--apply] [--auto] [--agent codex|claude]
                     [--codex-config <path>] [--claude-config <path>] [--json]
      Find agent configuration entries (Codex config.toml, ~/.claude.json,
      a repository's .mcp.json) that start a managed server without
      connect, or whose aethyme path is gone; --apply rewrites only those
      to the absolute aethyme (backup, compare-and-swap, formatting kept).
      --auto: only repair = "auto" entries, only the Codex TOML file. A
      tracked .mcp.json is only reported. Prints names, never values.
  aethyme mcp bypass [--json]
      Running processes that match a managed entry, counted per entry as
      via_connect, fallback or bypass (1 s budget; inconclusive when the
      process table cannot be read).
--- stderr
